data/92/qa/onboard.log:11:- **Kitchen housewares department (2206 cells, the big purple block).** "Kitchen" here labels a *shelf* department (Appliances, Cookware, Bake Ware, Dinnerware, Tabletop, Kitchen Gadgets/Cutlery wall run). The derived `auto:SEAFOOD` disk (r=130, bridge 20) reaches back to x=997, closed the 14‑18 pt gaps between shelf rows, and the pocket then held the KITCHEN label so the SERVICE_DEPTS substring cull condemned it as staff — the same false positive 658 and 660 hit.
data/92/qa/onboard.log:14:- **Baby cross-corridor west end (449-cell pocket at 337,423)** — swallowed by the `auto:PHARMACY` disk; the same 16 pt aisle stays open east of x=400 and carries the Baby Food / Baby Water shelf labels.
data/92/qa/onboard.log:20:2100 / 1920 / 870 / 228 cells = Lease Space rooms, restroom block, exit-side lease room (all raw-disconnected enclosed rooms); 480 + 313 + 421 = 4‑6 pt perimeter slivers behind the east wall shelving, the north Market gondolas and the produce wall cases; 165 = the seafood counter band itself; 138 = the 14 pt crevice between the Tortilleria wall and two display bins (no labels, leads nowhere).
data/92/qa/onboard.log:23:The derived `auto:CHECKSTANDS` rect leaves the checkout-lane slivers walkable: its bridging disconnects the whole SW quadrant in the sealed grid, which aisle-badge protection then keeps wholesale, lanes included. Every tighter seal rect I tried (left bank only, right bank only, both, bridges 14‑26) sealed the lanes but also killed the ~1800-cell post-checkout strip in front of the Business Center — real customer floor. I left the derived zone alone (`seal_zones: derived`); the one lane cell that *is* sealed, at (649,580), is in `must_not`. `far_snaps` for ENTRANCE/EXIT and PHARMACY DRIVE-THRU are the exterior door/lane labels printed outside the sales-floor boundary; SEAFOOD's new 2.8 m snap is the label moving to the customer side of its counter.
data/92/qa/onboard.log:25:Stopping here for the separate audit context as instructed — I have not run `docs/audit.md`.
